WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:09.680
Real data is often quite messy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.480
There are many possible issues with&nbsp;
real datasets such as bad formatting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:23.200
trailing spaces, duplicates, empty rows, synonyms&nbsp;
of different abbreviations, difference in scales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:31.520
inconsistency in description, skewed distributions&nbsp;
and outliers, and missing values. Each of these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:37.200
issues can cause problems in data analysis and&nbsp;
deserves attention in exploratory data analysis.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.840
There's substantial evidence&nbsp;
that data errors are widespread.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:50.080
In clinical research, the error rates&nbsp;
are reported to be between 2.3% to 26.9%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.520 --> 00:01:02.080
Sometimes, a seemingly minor mistake can cause&nbsp;
serious consequences. In 1999, NASA lost a $125M&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:07.680
Mars orbiter because an engineering team failed to&nbsp;
convert measurements from English to metric units.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:14.240
Data exploration is often the&nbsp;
first step in data analysis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:21.120
It involves summarizing and realizing the data&nbsp;
set. It should be done before any modeling work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.280
In addition to the detrimental consequences of&nbsp;
data errors as mentioned before, massive data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:33.440
can also cause modern issues. As an example, a&nbsp;
modern approach can fail if it encounters a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.840
numerical value for a numerical variable. This is&nbsp;
a case for the common yield regression analysis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.920
Also, analysis done on massive data can suffer&nbsp;
from the commonly known effect of garbage in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:53.840
garbage out. In other words, data analysis&nbsp;
done on faulty data can often be misleading.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:02:03.440
Data exploration serves several purposes. First,&nbsp;
it helps gain intuition about the data. Sometimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:09.840
this is called data understanding. Such intuition&nbsp;
can be quite useful in subsequent data analysis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:14.960
And they may even dictate an appropriate&nbsp;
choice of modeling techniques or tools.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:21.440
Second, it helps conduct sanity checks on the&nbsp;
data to ensure that a data looks reasonable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:27.120
in the right format and on the right scale.&nbsp;
Third, it helps find out whether there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:33.840
missing values outliers. Finally, it is common&nbsp;
to summarize the data in data exploration.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.160
These includes both the numeric&nbsp;
summary and data exploration.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:47.040
How should we perform data exploration? The&nbsp;
simplest way is to manually review the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.800
For those of you who have experienced working with&nbsp;
spreadsheets, you may have manually reviewed data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:03:00.320
in the past. However, manually reviewing data is&nbsp;
labor intensive and not feasible. Even an Excel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:07.440
spreadsheet can have as many 1 million rows. There&nbsp;
are several ways to explore large datasets. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.760
first, is to examine samples of data. By working&nbsp;
on samples of data we can directly interact with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.800
the raw data even for large datasets, which can&nbsp;
greatly help us to gain some sense on the data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:31.840
In the end, however, we need to summarize the data&nbsp;
either using numerical summary or data validation.

